serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>A post-Fascist pragmatist</span></h2><p style=
=3D"display:block;color:#333;font:400 18.5px/26px georgia,serif;margin:0 0 =
10px"><span>By Roger Cohen</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geor=
gia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Giorgia Meloni, the Italian pri=
me minister, has a vision.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px geor=
gia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Once, it was extracting Italy f=
rom the euro and the grip of the =E2=80=9Cusurers=E2=80=9D of the European =
Central Bank in order to steer her country toward a far-right nationalist f=
uture. But that was before power tempered her. Now rather than turning her =
back on Europe, she wants Italy to lead it.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norm=
al 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>When I spoke t=
o Meloni recently, she talked little of a sluggish economy, but a lot about=
 an Italy that =E2=80=9Cdoes not run after others.=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CI don=
=E2=80=99t like the idea of a submissive Italy,=E2=80=9D she told me. She w=
ants her country, the world=E2=80=99s eighth-largest economy, pivotally pla=
ced in the Mediterranean as the importance of Africa grows, to be =E2=80=9C=
a protagonist =E2=80=94 and one that can benefit all of Europe.=E2=80=9D</s=
pan></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0=
 0 15px"><span>(</span><span style=3D"font-style:italic">Read Roger=E2=80=
