#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Italy has found a long-elusive stability under =
her leadership. With President Emmanuel Macron of France departing next yea=
r, Meloni, who this week becomes the longest continually serving postwar pr=
ime minister of Italy, could emerge as the doyenne of European politics, a =
role once played by former Chancellor Angela Merkel of Germany. Of course, =
Merkel welcomed refugees; Meloni does not.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norma=
l 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>As she has jour=
neyed from post-Fascist roots to a pivotal place in European politics, Melo=
ni has remained something of an enigma. In our interview, she described Mus=
solini=E2=80=99s rule, which she considered an inspiration in her youth, as=
 a =E2=80=9Cparticularly complex=E2=80=9D time, even if the catastrophe of =
Il Duce=E2=80=99s dictatorship and his alliance with Hitler was clear enoug=
h by 1945.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:=
#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Her ambivalence over horrific history does not =
appear to affect her approach to the present, which is pragmatic.</span></p=
